Chapter: BoundedNoneAccepted
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Cost Model, the bottom rung, the arm that must COMPILE.
 `bounded-none-exceeded` in `codex/test/errors` proves the refusal; a refusal
 arm on its own cannot tell a working check from one that refuses everything,
 so this is the control.

 `adds` recurses, does arithmetic, compares, and calls `min`, which section
 3.1 measured at zero bytes. It allocates nothing and declares so. The two
 chapters differ in what the callee does with its accumulator: one pushes
 onto a list, this one adds an integer.

Section: A Callee That Allocates Nothing

  sum-to : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-to (n) (i) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else i + sum-to n (i + 1)

Section: The Declaration That Holds

  bounded none adds : Integer -> Integer
  adds (n) = sum-to n 0 + min n 4

Section: A Rung Widened By Measurement, Not By Rule

 `list-set-at` was on the refusing side until 2026-08-19 for one reason: it
 had never been measured. `codex/test/cost/builtin-alloc` measured it at 0
 bytes on a list of 64 and of 256, and `emit-list-set-at` turns out to have no
 allocation path in it at all -- a bounds check, an address, a store, and a
 return of the same pointer. Its `bs-alloc` row says `none` and this
 declaration holds because of that row.

 THIS ARM IS THE ONE THAT FAILS IF THE REGISTRY IS NOT CONSULTED. Every other
 accepted name here is also in `is-rt-safe-builtin`, so the whole chapter
 would still compile with the lookup removed; `list-set-at` is in the registry
 and not in that set, which is what makes it the discriminator.

  set-one : List Integer -> Integer
  set-one (xs) = list-length (list-set-at xs 0 9)

  bounded none seated : List Integer -> Integer
  seated (xs) = set-one xs

Section: Boolean `&` Allocates Nothing, And Is Now Recorded As Such

 `&` is one token doing two jobs, and the type recorded for the expression is
 the only thing that separates them afterwards. Until 2026-08-19 `infer-and`
 recorded on its TEXT arm and not its boolean one, so a boolean `&` looked
 exactly like an expression nothing knew the type of, and every reader of that
 table treats the unknown as the worst case. `bounded none` refused any body
 that joined two conditions, and `punctual` refused the same shape through
 `check-rt-no-alloc`; both were charging a boolean AND for a heap allocation
 it never makes.

 All three shapes are here because the defect was found by all three failing
 together, which is what ruled out the two theories that came first: it was
 not unresolved type variables and not parameter resolution, because
 `True & False` is as literal as an expression gets and it was refused too.

  bounded none joins-literals : Boolean
  joins-literals = True & False

  bounded none joins-params : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  joins-params (a) (b) = a & b

  bounded none joins-comparisons : Integer -> Boolean
  joins-comparisons (n) = n > 1 & n < 9

Section: A Ceiling Nothing Can Breach

 `growing` is the top of the lattice and accepts anything, including a body
 that allocates. It is here so the arm covers a satisfied ceiling as well as
 a satisfying body.

  bounded growing listed : Integer -> Integer
  listed (n) = list-length (list-push [] n)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("adds 8 = " & show (adds 8))
    print-line-uni ("listed 3 = " & show (listed 3))
    print-line-uni ("seated = " & show (seated [1, 2, 3]))
    print-line-uni ("joins = " & (if joins-literals then "t" else "f") & (if joins-params True True then "t" else "f") & (if joins-comparisons 5 then "t" else "f"))
  end
